// Heaven Benchmark — Falling rain precipitation streaks (Rain_Feature)

// Part of the weather system.
// Renders falling precipitation as camera-facing, vertically elongated streak
// quads inside a camera-centered bounded volume. Like the smoke / grass / splash
// materials, the geometry is built ONCE (one renderable of up to kMaxStreaks
// (20000) streak quads) and every per-streak position + length is computed in
// the vertex shader from a per-streak seed and a handful of uniforms — zero CPU
// work per frame after the buffer is built once. The CPU never
// moves a vertex; it only pushes uniforms.

// UNIFICATION: a streak is now a DROP ON THE SHARED LATTICE, not an arbitrary
// hash3 cell with its own fall term. Each streak slot maps to a world-anchored
// lattice cell around the camera; its world XY is that cell's jittered column
// (drop_column_xy) and its Z is the shared fall law (drop_z). The splash shader
// derives the SAME cell from a ground pixel and the SAME fall law, so a streak
// column and the splash cell it lands in agree byte-for-byte.

// The math here MIRRORS engine/weather_shader_ref.h (namespace
// heaven::weather_ref) byte-for-byte — that header is the authoritative CPU
// reference and the property-based tests assert the two stay in lockstep:

//     world-anchored lattice cell for this seed (streak_seed_cell):
//     float3 h     = hash3(seed);
//     float  seedX = (h.x*2 - 1) * E;   float seedY = (h.y*2 - 1) * E;
//     float  relX  = mod(seedX - camAbs.x + E, 2E) - E; nearest tiled instance
//     float  relY  = mod(seedY - camAbs.y + E, 2E) - E;
//     float2 cell  = floor(vec2(camAbs.x+relX, camAbs.y+relY) / kDropSpacing);
//     shared fall law (fixed span 2E, per-cell speed) — drop_z:
//     float  fallPeriod = (2E/fallSpeed) * mix(0.8, 1.4, hash21(cell+29.7));
//     float  vSpeed     = 2E / fallPeriod; drop_fall_speed
//     float  cellPhase  = hash21(cell+5.3) * fallPeriod; drop_phase
//     float  z          = (camAbs.z+E) - vSpeed*mod(time-cellPhase, fallPeriod);
//     jittered column world XY (drop_column_xy):
//     float2 colXY = (cell + hash22(cell*1.37+3.1)) * kDropSpacing;
//     vec3   absWorld = vec3(dropXY, z); dropXY = colXY + wind drift
//     vec3   world    = absWorld - camAbs + camShading; map to shading space
//     float  len      = mix(streakLenMin, streakLenMax, hash1(seed.x));

//   - WORLD-ANCHORING: relX/relY wrap the tiled instance of this seed nearest
//     the ABSOLUTE camera into [-E, E), so the snapped lattice cell holds its
//     world position as the camera moves (camera flies THROUGH the rain) and
//     wraps by one window when the seed drifts a full span away (active set
//     stays centered on the camera, density constant —).
//   - BOUNDED, CAMERA-CENTERED: the fall span is FIXED at exactly 2E
//     (vSpeed*fallPeriod == 2E), so z = drop_z sweeps (camAbs.z - E, camAbs.z + E]
//     — strictly inside the camera-centered 140-unit cube — and only the per-cell
//     SPEED varies (the approved design tradeoff: vary speed, not span). The XY
//     column lies within one kDropSpacing cell of the wrapped [-E, E) band.
//   - `mod(..., 2E)` / `mod(time-cellPhase, fallPeriod)` are GLSL
//     `mod(x,y)=x-y*floor(x/y)`, exactly the reference header's glsl_mod (NOT a
//     sign-preserving fmod).
//   - FRAME-RATE INDEPENDENT: the cell + column carry no time dependence
//     so XY is bit-identical across cadences; z is a pure function of the
//     absolute `time`, so the distance fallen per real second is frame-rate
//     independent.
//   - The streak length is in [0.12, 0.7] world units, driven by hash1,
//     and each drop also gets a per-drop brightness + width jitter so drops
//     differ (appearance only).
//   - WIND: a slow time-varying horizontal gust (kWindDir, windStrength(time))
//     drifts each drop downwind by an amount that grows with the distance it has
//     fallen (dropXY = colXY + kWindDir*windStrength*(dropTopZ - z)); the streak
//     billboard is elongated along the drop's VELOCITY (down + wind) so slanted
//     rain reads as slanted. The SAME wind model is applied by heaven_splash.mat
//     (at z = groundZ, strength at impact) so a slanted drop splashes exactly
//     where it lands. At windStrength 0 this reduces to the pure vertical fall.

// TWO CAMERA REFERENCES (shader) vs ONE (header) — read carefully:
//   Filament shades in CAMERA-RELATIVE world space (the default; no
//   setCameraRelative override). So material.worldPosition and the clip
//   matrices live in a SHADING space translated so the camera sits near the
//   origin. The proven, non-drifting placement is therefore to ADD the
//   SHADING-space camera `frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[3].xyz` (camShading)
//   for the final world position — that is why the volume correctly surrounds
//   the camera.
//   But to make drops appear WORLD-STATIC, the per-drop OFFSET must be anchored
//   to the ABSOLUTE camera world position, still available as the pushed uniform
//   `materialParams.cameraPos`. The identity `camShading = cameraPos + const`
//   (constant per frame) means using cameraPos for the wrap/anchor terms and
//   camShading for the final translate makes the drop world-static regardless of
//   which convention Filament uses: as cameraPos moves by Δ the offset moves by
//   −Δ (mod the window), cancelling the camera's motion.
//   The CPU reference header has no notion of two camera spaces, so it adds its
//   single `cameraPos` param for BOTH the anchor terms and the final placement —
//   that is expected; the OFFSET expressions (cell, relX/relY, fallen, relZ) are
//   byte-for-byte identical, which is all the property tests check.

// World convention is Z-up (the Heaven world): the streak is elongated along
// the drop's VELOCITY direction (world -Z plus the horizontal wind drift), and
// the quad is rotated about that axis to face the camera (billboard) so the flat
// face always points at the viewer. With no wind the long axis is world
// +Z (pure vertical), exactly as before.

// Streaks whose slot is inactive (streakIndex >= activeStreaks) or that occur at
// rain intensity 0 collapse to a degenerate point so they render nothing.

// Vertex attributes (one quad = 4 vertices), matching design.md:
//   POSITION.xyz = unused base (the world position is computed in the shader)
//   UV0.xy       = corner id in [0,1] (x = across the streak, y = along it)
//   CUSTOM0.xyzw = (seed.x, seed.y, seed.z, streakIndex)
//                  seed.xyz is the per-streak seed; the 4th ("spare") slot
//                  carries the streak's slot index, used for the activeStreaks
//                  cull. RainFeature::build_rain  fills these once.

// Material parameters (pushed per frame by RainFeature::update —).
// This is exactly the implementation.md RainFeature::update streak uniform list:
//   time: float  — global animation time (getUserTime(), seconds)
//   rainIntensity: float  — effective rain intensity in [0,1] (0 => nothing)
//   activeStreaks: int    — round(intensity * kMaxStreaks); slots >= this collapse
//   cameraPos: float3 — world-space camera position (volume centre)
//   volumeExtent: float  — half-extent of the camera-centered cube (70.0)
//   fallSpeed: float  — world units fallen per second
//   streakLenMin: float  — minimum streak length (0.12)
//   streakLenMax: float  — maximum streak length (0.7)

// volumeExtent is a uniform (default/pushed 70.0) so the camera-centered cube
// tracks the C++ kVolumeExtent used by weather_shader_ref / RainFeature.

// Compiled with: matc -a opengl -p desktop -o heaven_rain.filamat heaven_rain.mat

material {
    name : heaven_rain,
    requires : [ uv0, custom0 ],
    shadingModel : unlit,
    blending : transparent,
    doubleSided : true,
    flipUV : false,
    // Falling rain is occluded by closer scene geometry (depth test on, the
    // default) but must not write depth, so transparent streaks behind it still
    // composite correctly — same convention as the smoke particles.
    depthWrite : false,
    parameters : [
        { type : float,  name : time },
        { type : float,  name : rainIntensity },
        { type : int,    name : activeStreaks },
        { type : float3, name : cameraPos, precision : high },
        { type : float,  name : volumeExtent },
        { type : float,  name : fallSpeed },
        { type : float,  name : streakLenMin },
        { type : float,  name : streakLenMax },
        { type : float4, name : rainShaderTuning }
    ],
    variables : [
        // .x = accept flag (1 = active streak, 0 = collapsed) — fades the
        //      fragment so inactive / non-raining streaks draw nothing.
        // .y = per-drop brightness/alpha scale in ~[0.5, 1.0] (a pure function
        //      of the per-streak seed) so drops differ in brightness — the
        //      fragment multiplies its alpha by this.
        streakParams
    ]
}

vertex {
    #include "includes/effect_rain_vertex.glsl"
    void materialVertex(inout MaterialVertexInputs material) {
        effectVertex(material, materialParams.time,
            float3(frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[0].xyz),
            float3(frameUniforms.worldFromViewMatrix[1].xyz),
            getWorldCameraPosition(), materialParams.cameraPos);
    }
}

fragment {
    #include "includes/effect_rain_fragment.glsl"
    void material(inout MaterialInputs material) { effectShade(material); }
}
